        "heading": "Football: league position and cup progress",
        "content": "The completed 2024/25 football table offers a useful introduction to the geography of Iranian clubs. Tractor, recorded as Teraktor-Sazi in the statistical archive, finished above Sepahan of Isfahan and Persepolis of Tehran. The same table includes Foolad Khuzestan, Malavan of Bandar-e Anzali, and clubs from Sirjan, Arak, Qazvin, and Rafsanjan. It is a national competition whose participants extend well beyond the capital. [s1]\n\nThe Hazfi Cup should be read alongside the league rather than folded into its standings. The archive records it through successive elimination rounds. A team's cup progress and its league position consequently describe different competitive achievements. This distinction also matters when interpreting headlines: winning a cup final does not establish that a club led the league. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "volleyball",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Volleyball: a domestic final and a continental stage",
        "content": "Volleyball provides a different model of deciding a domestic title. The Asian Volleyball Confederation's report on the 2023/24 Iran Super League describes a best-of-three championship final between Foolad Sirjan and Shahdab Yazd, won by Foolad. Its final classification also lists Paykan and Urmia jointly in third place. A final series and shared placing should be preserved as reported, rather than translated into an assumed football-style table. [s2]\n\nThe two finalists subsequently met in the Asian Men's Club Championship final in Yazd in September 2024. Volleyball World reported that Foolad won the continental title and that both Iranian teams qualified for that year's Men's Club World Championship. These were club teams representing themselves in international competition, distinct from Iran's national team. [s3]\n\nThe example also shows why competition levels need explicit names. A domestic championship, an Asian club championship, and a world club championship are separate events. Success in one can open a route to another under the rules of that edition, but their results should never be combined into a single undifferentiated title count. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "university",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "University sport has its own history",
        "content": "FISU records Iran's membership from 1967 and identifies Tehran as the host of the second World University Wrestling Championship in 1996. These entries establish a documented international university-sport connection and a specific hosting milestone. They do not, by themselves, describe the present domestic university competition calendar or demonstrate how athletes move between universities and professional clubs. [s4]\n\nFor readers following this branch, the key distinction is the setting in which participation takes place. A university event should be identified through its student-sport organizer and event rules; a professional club result should be attributed to the relevant club competition. The shared presence of a sport such as wrestling does not make these institutional settings interchangeable. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "eligibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Results are only part of continental eligibility",
        "content": "Continental football participation also involves licensing. In January 2022, the Asian Football Confederation withdrew the Champions League licences of Persepolis, Esteghlal, and Gol Gohar Sirjan because mandatory criteria had not been satisfied. The announcement establishes a decision for that edition; it should not be presented as a permanent exclusion. [s5]\n\nTogether, these examples provide a practical way to read Iranian competitions: identify the team, the event, the season, and the governing decision before comparing achievements. This overview remains incomplete for domestic wrestling competitions and the broader national-championship system; those branches require additional federation documentation.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
