    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Football Clubs: Identities and Regional Rivals | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Meet major Iranian football clubs through their histories, regional identities, recent titles, and the distinction between results and continental licensing."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "tehran-identities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Start with identities, not a single league table",
        "content": "Iranian club football is easier to understand when a club's identity is separated from its position in a particular season. Esteghlal and Persepolis are the two teams in the Tehran derby, the capital's prominent football rivalry. The Asian Football Confederation's historical account traces Esteghlal to a cycling club founded in 1945 as Docharkhe Savaran, meaning 'The Cyclists'. Football became one of its activities later. [s1]\n\nThe same account explains successive names: Taj, meaning 'Crown', was adopted in 1949, and Esteghlal, meaning 'Independence', in 1979. Older records using Taj therefore belong to the history of the club now called Esteghlal. This is a useful caution when comparing historical honours: a change of name does not necessarily mark the creation of a different club. [s1]\n\nThe derby is an entry point rather than a complete map of Iranian football. Its prominence should not obscure clubs elsewhere, or encourage treating every national championship as a contest decided exclusively within Tehran.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-competition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Look beyond the capital",
        "content": "The historical championship archive records league-winning clubs from several centres. Sepahan of Isfahan, Foolad of Ahvaz, and Esteghlal Khuzestan of Ahvaz have all won the professional league. These examples establish that national success has repeatedly extended outside Tehran, although a list of champions cannot measure the size or composition of each club's support. [s2]\n\nTractor of Tabriz added a new chapter in 2025. The AFC reported its first Iran Pro League title after victory over Shams Azar. The club appears as Teraktor-Sazi in RSSSF's season table, illustrating another practical issue for international readers: English transliterations and shortened names vary across sources. [s3][s4]\n\nThat completed table places Tractor first, Sepahan second, Persepolis third, and Esteghlal ninth. It captures a particular season rather than a permanent hierarchy. Reputation, historical honours, and current league position answer different questions and should be presented separately. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "licensing-and-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A club's institutional record also matters",
        "content": "Sporting performance is not the only condition affecting Asian competition. The AFC's January 2022 announcement withdrew the Champions League licences of Persepolis, Esteghlal, and Gol Gohar Sirjan after finding that mandatory criteria had not been met. That finding concerns eligibility for the named edition; it does not establish a permanent prohibition or justify attributing an unreported cause to every club. [s5]\n\nFor a first encounter with Iranian clubs, use names and locations to establish identity, season tables to assess performance, and dated regulatory decisions to understand eligibility. The examples here introduce major clubs without ranking their supporters or assuming that historical institutional affiliations describe current ownership.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
