    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Gulf Pro League: Seasons and Title Races | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's leading men's football league through five completed seasons, sourced champions' points, and explicit limits on current-season evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A season-based national competition",
        "content": "The Persian Gulf Pro League is encountered under several English names, including Iran Pro League and Iran Premier/Pro League. RSSSF's historical archive separates the professional competition beginning in 2001/02 from earlier national league structures. Historical title totals therefore need a stated scope: all national championships and the professional-era league are not identical categories. [s1]\n\nA league season also differs from a calendar year. The labels 2020/21 and 2024/25 refer to competitions spanning parts of successive years. They should remain season labels when charted, rather than being converted into apparently annual January-to-December observations. The comparison below uses completed editions and the final points reported for their champions. [s2][s3][s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "title-races",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What completed tables reveal",
        "content": "Persepolis led the 2020/21 table, followed by Sepahan. Esteghlal then won the 2021/22 edition without a recorded league defeat. Persepolis returned to first place in the next edition and retained the championship in 2023/24. That latter table places Esteghlal immediately behind, illustrating how the final ordering can distinguish clubs with very similar season-long records. [s2][s3][s4][s5]\n\nTractor, listed as Teraktor-Sazi in RSSSF, finished first in 2024/25. The AFC independently reported that this was the club's first Iran Pro League championship. The sequence shows changes in the identity of the winner even when champions' final points remain within a narrow range. Points alone cannot explain the tactical, financial, or organizational reasons behind those changes. [s6][s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "champion-points-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Champions' final league points, 2020/21–2024/25",
          "dataset_id": "champion-points",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["winner-points"],
          "x_axis_label": "League season",
          "y_axis_label": "League points",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Across these five completed seasons, champions finished with 66–68 points. The changing winner is identified in each observation's note; this is not a single club's performance trend."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "champion-points-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Completed-season champions and their final points",
          "dataset_id": "champion-points"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keep results, eligibility, and incomplete seasons separate",
        "content": "League placement is a sporting result; continental eligibility also depends on regulation. The AFC's withdrawal of Champions League licences from Persepolis, Esteghlal, and Gol Gohar Sirjan in January 2022 demonstrates why a domestic table cannot establish eligibility by itself. The announcement should be read as a dated ruling for that competition edition. [s8]\n\nA further caution concerns unfinished competitions. RSSSF's 2025/26 record labels the league abandoned and explicitly states that no championship was awarded. Its table contains unequal match totals and is labelled a table at abandonment. It cannot be appended to the chart as another completed champion's season. The reported abandonment requires corroboration from a dated federation or league decision before this page can offer a fully verified account through the editorial cutoff. [s9]\n\nFor now, the completed-season comparison supports a bounded conclusion: championship points were similar across the selected editions, while the winning club changed. It does not establish current standings, the latest competition rules, or a long-run trend in competitive balance.",
        "source_ids": ["s8", "s9"]
      }
    ],
